there's something so soothing so  familiar about clicking on monsters  until they fall over dead Grim Dawn  provides the low-risk high-reward kind  of gameplay isometric action  role-playing games typically deliver  even as an early access purchase on  Steam for $29.99 its development studio  is humbled by Arthur Bruno the lead  systems designer of the well-regarded  click fest Titan Quest making an early  purchase of Grim Dawn difficult to  resist  at very least Grim Dawn has the basics  down it's dark demonic world mixes guns  swords and magic into a greatest hits  compilation of role-playing tropes you  choose the armor and the weapons that  best suit your playstyle and then head  out in search of fame and fortune as is  so often the case with these kinds of  games the banal story is just an excuse  to get you moving but where Grim Dawn  might have made a mark by depicting a  unique world it's perfectly content to  be just another Fantasy Kingdom in a  genre where path of Exile torchlight 2  and even a Camaro were recognizable  on-site Grim Dawn sticks with the dark  fantasy creatures were used to and  depicts a generically attractive land  that doesn't evoke any mood in  particular but make no mistake Grim Dawn  is attractive and it executes the old  formula as best as can be expected with  just enough panache to keep you clicking  the open-ended class system lets you  explore the talent trees of two  different professions of your choice so  if you've ever wanted to be a pistol  wielding creature summoner here's your  chance  further skills are afforded by rare  equipment that grants you even more  abilities and I found myself mixing and  matching skills in ways I didn't  initially intend mix and match character  builds aren't always effective in games  like this but I never felt particularly  challenged at least not until I made my  way to grim Dawn's final extensive  dungeon it's difficult for the developer  of a Diablo s role-playing game to grab  your attention with 29.99 you aren't  just buying an early access game but  also making a statement of trust in its  maker  Grim Dawn comes with a sturdy foundation  and with lots of features coming in the  future like cooperative play and  additional acts the game is likely to be  fleshed out and supported for some time  to come that's just as well considering  you only get about six to eight hours of  adventuring in the current build  question is then whether Grim Dawn can  gain an identity of its own given how  much of what Grim Dawn does is done just  as well elsewhere and with a little bit  more flavor that's not an absolute given
 

